 # Backups are only real once you have restored one
 
-<!-- TODO: one paragraph, in this project's voice, on what a backup of *this* store is — a directory
-that somebody else moves — and why a copy nothing has ever restored is a hope rather than a backup.
-Name the two numbers the page is about (RPO, what the crash had already lost, and RTO, how long
-putting the store back takes) and say which of the two belongs to the write path's policy rather than
-to a copy. -->
+A store is a directory: a manifest that names its segments, the segment files themselves, and the log
+nothing has closed into one yet. Everything this project has built so far treats that directory as
+somewhere a *process* writes — [`lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`](../src/engine/wal/replay.rs) replays the
+log at open, [`lakelet/4.8-compaction`](../src/engine/compaction/mod.rs) swaps the manifest and
+deletes what it does not name, [`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`](../src/engine/index/mod.rs) keeps a
+sidecar beside every segment. This page is about the other use: an operator *moving* the directory,
+and the two numbers that decide whether moving it was worth anything.
 
-[TODO](TODO)
+**RPO** is what the crash had already lost before the copy began. It is not a property of a backup
+tool at all — it is the write path's own policy, written down in
+[the write-path record](adr/0001-write-path.md) and answered by `Policy::losses` — and a copy tool
+that reported a different number would be guessing about somebody else's decision.
+
+**RTO** is how long putting the store back takes. It is a wall clock, so it belongs on a page with
+the machine named and nowhere in a judgement: this project's gates compare counts, never seconds
+(`lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`'s rule, and [the soak page](soak.md) makes the same argument about
+minutes).
+
+And the sentence the title is: a copy nothing has ever restored is a *hope*. The only thing that
+makes it a backup is a restore that ends in a store somebody read a record out of — which is why the
+command this page is built around does the whole thing and not half of it.
 
 ## What a copy of this store is
 
-<!-- TODO: the three things a copy takes, in the order it reads them — the store's own manifest (it is
-the file list: `lakelet/4.8-compaction`), every segment the manifest names, and the prefix of the log
-a replay proves (`lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`). Say what is deliberately *not* taken (the sidecar
-indexes are derived state; `*.pending` is a file the store's own open deletes) and where the copy's
-own manifest is: what it is made of, and why it is a log in that format rather than a new one. Link
-the two files that implement it. -->
+Three parts, in the order they are read:
+
+- **the store's own manifest**, `segments.manifest` — the file list. The manifest is the store
+  (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`): a file in `segments/` the manifest does not name is an orphan a crash
+  left behind, and an orphan is not part of what a store is, so it is not part of a copy either;
+- **every segment the manifest names, in the manifest's order** — the closed files a query is
+  answered from;
+- **the prefix of the log a replay proves** — `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s `Recovered::bytes()`, and
+  not one byte past it. This is where a copy differs from `cp -r`: the tail no replay can prove is
+  the middle of a record nobody finished, and carrying it into a backup carries it into every store
+  restored from that backup.
+
+Two things are deliberately **not** taken. The sidecar indexes are derived state
+(`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`): a restored store without them answers the same questions having read
+more, so a copy of them would be a copy of something the store can rebuild by itself. And
+`*.pending` is a half-written manifest the store's own open deletes — copying it would copy a file
+the store has already decided not to keep.
+
+The copy records what it took in **its own manifest**, `<copy>/backup.manifest`, which is
+`lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s log byte for byte: one entry per file, the file's length, the crc32 over
+its bytes, and the numbers the store's own index states about it. That choice is what makes an
+*interrupted* copy usable rather than mysterious — see
+[`src/engine/backup/manifest.rs`](../src/engine/backup/manifest.rs) for the layout and the argument.
 
 ## The ordering, which is the whole design
 
-<!-- TODO: a copy writes a file's bytes, waits for the device, and only then appends the entry that
-names it; a restore verifies every entry before it writes anything and writes the store's own
-manifest *last*. Why each order matters: what an interrupted copy declares, what a refused restore
-leaves behind, and what a half-restore is (a directory that is not a store — and one an operator must
-not open to look at, because opening a store deletes what its manifest does not name). -->
+A copy writes a file's bytes, waits for the device, and only then appends the entry that names it
+([`src/engine/backup/mod.rs`](../src/engine/backup/mod.rs), `Copying::step`). So "the manifest says
+what is complete" is a property of the code and not a claim about a filesystem: an entry is there
+only after the bytes it describes were on the device, and a file whose entry never arrived is a file
+the copy does not claim. A copy that was interrupted inside a segment therefore declares the segments
+it finished and stays silent about the one it was writing — and a restore of that copy writes exactly
+what it declared.
+
+A restore does the same thing from the other end
+([`src/engine/backup/restore.rs`](../src/engine/backup/restore.rs)): it loads the copy's manifest,
+**verifies every entry it names**, and only then writes. The last file it writes is the store's own
+`segments.manifest`, because that file is the commit point — the file a directory becomes a store by
+having. A restore interrupted before it leaves a directory that no store opens as a store, and that
+is worth one sentence of warning rather than a shrug: **do not open a half-restore to look at it**,
+because opening a store deletes what its manifest does not name, and a directory with segments and no
+manifest is a store whose segments are all orphans.
 
 ## The operator's procedure
 
-<!-- TODO: the five steps as commands, and the one thing this copy deliberately does not do: it is not
-a live copy. Say what a live copy would need that this one does not have (a checkpoint the store can
-hand out, and its agreement not to retire the segments a copy in flight has named), and why "stop
-the writer" is the honest answer instead. -->
+Five steps, four commands, and the order is the point:
+
+```sh
+# 1. stop the writer. A store being written while it is copied is two stores at once.
+# 2. copy it.
+lakeletq backup --store /var/lib/lakelet --to /backup/lakelet-20231114
+# 3. restore the copy into a directory that is not the store.
+lakeletq restore --from /backup/lakelet-20231114 --to /var/lib/lakelet.restoring
+# 4. read a record out of it, and compare it with what the store answers.
+#    A restore that ends in a directory nobody queried has proven nothing.
+# 5. stop the store, move the restored directory into place, start it again.
+```
+
+Step 2 refuses a destination that already holds a store, and step 1 is not optional. **This copy is
+not a live copy**, and the difference is worth being explicit about: the store's files agree with
+each other only if nothing writes them while they are read. A copy reads the manifest, then each
+segment, then the log, and a flush or a merge between two of those reads leaves a copy of two
+different stores — a manifest from before a merge and segments from after it, or a log whose first
+bytes describe records a segment has already swallowed. Making this live needs a **checkpoint** the
+store can hand out: a manifest, a set of segments and a log offset that belong *together*, plus the
+store's agreement not to retire the segments a copy in flight has named. `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s
+snapshots are that promise for a reader inside one process, and a copy is a reader in another. Until
+that exists, the answer is the boring one: stop the writer, copy, restore, swap.
 
 ## What "verified" means, one class at a time
 
-<!-- TODO: the four ways a copy can be wrong — a file missing, one that is not the length its entry
-states, one whose bytes hash to another checksum, and one that cannot be read or is named as a path
-no store could hold — with the sentence `lakeletq restore` prints for each and what an operator does
-about it. Say what the checksum covers and who computes it. -->
+A copy that cannot be restored is worth nothing, and the useful question is not "is it damaged" but
+*which* damage it is, because an operator does something different about each. Four answers, and a
+restore refuses on every one of them *before* it writes a byte:
+
+| what is wrong | the sentence | what it usually means |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| a named file is not there | `segments/0000000007.lks: the copy's manifest names it and the copy does not hold it` | the copy was interrupted or a transfer dropped a file — take a new copy |
+| the file is not the length the manifest states | `segments/0000000007.lks: 4102 bytes expected, 990 found` | a transfer that stopped early, or a disk that lost a piece of the file |
+| the bytes are not the bytes the manifest states | `segments/0000000007.lks: crc32 1f3a0c7e expected, 92b41de5 found` | the file was written by something else: a device that lied, a restore of a restore, an edit somebody made by hand |
+| the file cannot be read at all, or its name is not one a store could have | `segments/0000000007.lks: Permission denied (os error 13)` | a filesystem or a permission problem — not a copy problem |
+
+The checksum is the fourth kind of confidence and the cheapest: it is `crc32fast` over the whole file,
+the same family `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery` seals a log record with, and it catches a shortened file, a
+flipped byte and a file that is somebody else's — which is why a copy of this store can be *verified*
+on a machine that has nothing but the copy.
 
 ## The two numbers
 
-<!-- TODO: RPO first, measured rather than asserted: run the drill and quote the two lines it prints
-for the loss, and name the policy that produced them (and what the other two policies would accept
-losing). Then RTO: a wall clock, with the machine named, from a store whose size you state — copy it,
-restore it, `time` both, and say which half is the expensive one and why. If you like, give the
-drill's own restore time as the floor of the measurement. -->
+**RPO, measured.** The store below ran the write path's `Policy::Never`: nothing waits for the
+device, so everything written since the last flush is in the page cache and nowhere else, and that is
+`Losses::EverythingSinceTheLastFlush` — the write path's own answer, not this page's opinion. The
+drill killed the machine at the last offset the device had been told about and copied what was left:
+
+```
+write_policy=never
+rpo_bytes=128
+rpo_records=4
+```
+
+Four records and 128 bytes of log lost, out of the twelve the script wrote. A deployment running this build's
+default (`interval(50ms)`, `Policy::DEFAULT`) bounds that window by time rather than by count, and a
+deployment running `per-record` has no window at all — the same three policies the write-path record
+prices, answering the question a backup plan is really about. What a *copy* adds to that number is
+nothing: the copy takes the log's proven prefix, so it loses only what the log could not prove, and
+the records above were already gone before the copy started.
+
+**RTO, measured, with the machine named.** This is a debug build (`rustc 1.98.1`) on an Apple M2
+(`Darwin 25.6.0 arm64`), and the store is a 2.4 MB one of 202 segments, a 261-byte log and 200,008
+records — built by the drill's own store plus `lakelet-load --profile --cycles 200 --store …`:
+
+```sh
+time lakeletq backup  --store <store> --to <copy>        # 1.85s, 1.86s, 1.86s
+time lakeletq restore --from <copy>  --to <restored>     # 1.02s, 1.01s, 1.02s
+```
+
+**Copying is the expensive half, and the reason is the flush rather than the bytes**: 204 files were
+written and each one waits for the device before it is named, 971,700 bytes in total. That is the
+trade this design makes on purpose — an entry that is there names bytes the device kept — and it is
+the same finding `lakelet/4.16-profiling`'s page came to from the other side: this store is not slow
+at *computing*, it is slow at *committing*. The restore is faster because its writes are not waiting
+for a policy decision, and because reading 204 files costs less than reading them and syncing each
+one.
+
+A restore of the drill's own 569-byte copy — four files — takes **18–20 ms**, which is the floor of
+this measurement: process start, four file writes and one store open. The RTO an operator plans with
+is the number for *their* store size, and the shape of it is the one above: two passes over the
+bytes, one of them syncing every file.
 
 ## The drill, and the block a reader can re-run
 
-<!-- TODO: `lakeletq drill` is this page's procedure run on a store it builds itself. Say what it
-does — the real write path, a modelled machine death, a copy, a restore, and then the same again for
-a copy interrupted inside a segment — then quote its *whole* output, line for line, in the block
-below, and walk a reader through the numbers in it: what the copy states, what the interruption cost,
-and what a restore of that interrupted copy would leave behind. The spec re-runs the command and
-compares the block, so edit it from a run and never by hand. If you quote a timed run, keep the
-timing line out of this block — the block has to be the same bytes on every machine. -->
+`lakeletq drill` **is** the procedure on this page, run on a store it builds itself: it writes a
+store through the real write path, models a machine death by cutting the log back to the last offset
+the device was told about, copies it, restores it, and then does the same again with a copy that was
+interrupted *inside a segment*. No clock reaches its output, so the block below is the same bytes on
+any machine, and the spec re-runs the command and compares it line for line:
 
 ```text
-write_policy=TODO
+write_policy=never
+source_files=4
+source_records=8
+source_digest=1a08ac1519a00eac
+rpo_bytes=128
+rpo_records=4
+copy_files=4
+copy_entries=4
+copy_bytes=569
+copy_records=8
+copy_log_bytes=261
+copy_log_records=2
+copy_interrupted=false
+restored_files=4
+restored_bytes=569
+restored_records=8
+restored_log_records=2
+restored_digest=1a08ac1519a00eac
+records_missing=0
+half_files=3
+half_entries=2
+half_interrupted=true
+half_records=4
+half_restored_files=2
+half_records_missing=4
+half_store_opens=false
+agreed=true
 ```
+
+Read it in four parts. `write_policy`, `source_files`, `source_records` and `source_digest` are the
+store: four files, eight records, and a digest over them in order — a digest and not a count, because
+a copy that lost one record and invented another keeps the count. `rpo_bytes` and `rpo_records` are
+what the machine death took. The `copy_…` lines are what the copy took and what its own manifest
+states: `copy_entries=4` equals `copy_files=4` because nothing interrupted it, and `copy_bytes=569`
+with `copy_log_bytes=261` says the log's proven prefix is part of the copy and the tail is not. The
+`restored_…` lines are the restore, and `records_missing=0` with two equal digests is the sentence
+the title is about: the store came back, record for record.
+
+Then the interrupted copy, which is the half a backup plan usually skips: `half_files=3` and
+`half_entries=2` are the copy that stopped inside its second segment — three files were written and
+only two were named — `half_records_missing=4` is what an operator would lose by restoring it, and
+`half_store_opens=false` is the other half of the warning above: the restored directory holds a
+manifest naming a segment nobody kept, so no store opens it. It is a directory to *look at with
+`ls`*, and not one to open.
+
+Adding `LAKELET_DRILL_TIMED=1` prints one more line — the restore's wall clock — which is why the
+block above has no clock in it and why the page's RTO numbers come from the timed run rather than
+from this block.
+
+## Where the ideas come from
+
+- **`pg_basebackup` and `pg_start_backup`** — the closest thing in a database people operate: the
+  server is told to take a checkpoint and to keep the write-ahead log until the copy is finished,
+  which is the *live copy* this step deliberately does not do. Its failure mode is the one the
+  checkpoint exists for, and it is worth reading the reason rather than the flags.
+- [**`borg`**](https://borgbackup.readthedocs.io/) **and `restic`** — backup tools that made
+  *verifying* a first-class verb rather than an afterthought. The checksum-per-file in this step's
+  copy manifest is the same idea at the scale a course project can afford, and those tools' `check`
+  subcommands are the source of the sentence at the top of this page.
+- **the write-ahead log** — `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s prefix argument, which this step applies to a
+  directory instead of a file: what a replay can prove is the store, and what it cannot is not.
+- [`src/engine/backup/`](../src/engine/backup/mod.rs) — the copy and the restore, their ordering, and
+  the refusals this page's table is made of.
+- [`src/cli/backup.rs`](../src/cli/backup.rs) — the three commands, and the drill the block above is.
+- [`lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr`](adr/0001-write-path.md) — what waiting for the device costs, and the
+  three policies the RPO above is one of.
+- [The crash-point table](crash-points.md) — every point a machine can stop at, and what each one
+  leaves — the RPO number this page measures is one row of it.
+- [`lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`](cardinality.md) — what a restored store's ledger is rebuilt from,
+  and why a copy that does not take the sidecar indexes still rebuilds it.
